WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.086
Drift is a difference between the policy
library and a device's reported state that

2
00:00:05.086 --> 00:00:09.392
the policy should have prevented:
BitLocker off, tamper protection off,

3
00:00:09.392 --> 00:00:12.312
an OS below the floor, a
setting stuck in Conflict.

4
00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:16.341
The program detects it with the
same loop Chapter 05 describes

5
00:00:16.341 --> 00:00:19.750
for the governed path, applied
to Intune and Defender.

6
00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:24.350
A daily export of compliance,
configuration and inventory status.

7
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:28.000
A comparison against the
template values in Chapter 08.

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.325
Classification as a policy
gap, a device fault,

9
00:00:31.325 --> 00:00:35.438
a conflict or an inventory
mismatch, alerted by severity.

10
00:00:35.438 --> 00:00:38.981
And remediation: a device
sync or a Remediation script,

11
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:43.863
or a policy change through change
control when the library itself is wrong.

12
00:00:43.863 --> 00:00:48.412
On the governed path every remediating
write carries an approval reference.

13
00:00:48.412 --> 00:00:51.163
Know what Log Analytics
receives, and when.

14
00:00:51.163 --> 00:00:55.345
Intune's diagnostic settings send
audit logs, operational logs,

15
00:00:55.345 --> 00:00:59.763
compliance organizational logs
and the Intune devices inventory.

16
00:00:59.763 --> 00:01:02.738
Audit and operational logs
are sent immediately.

17
00:01:02.738 --> 00:01:08.047
Compliance and inventory are exported once
every twenty-four hours and can take up

18
00:01:08.047 --> 00:01:13.049
to forty-eight to arrive, and Microsoft
says not to rely on a fixed delivery time.

19
00:01:13.049 --> 00:01:17.188
So those tables are for trends and
evidence, not minute-level alerts.

20
00:01:17.188 --> 00:01:20.404
And per-setting configuration
status isn't among them,

21
00:01:20.404 --> 00:01:24.550
so the configuration comparison
comes from Microsoft Graph reports.

22
00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:28.441
Three query pitfalls: don't
hard-code compliance state strings,

23
00:01:28.441 --> 00:01:32.557
don't join the operational logs on
a device ID column it doesn't have,

24
00:01:32.557 --> 00:01:35.625
and don't look for
configuration conflicts there.

25
00:01:35.625 --> 00:01:37.200
The alerts follow from that.

26
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.612
Fleet-wide noncompliance above ten
percent comes from the daily export.

27
00:01:41.612 --> 00:01:45.355
But Tier 0 noncompliance is
polled from Graph hourly:

28
00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:49.012
from the export, the alert
would arrive two days late.

29
00:01:49.012 --> 00:01:55.940
Meanwhile Conditional Access blocks the
device, because CP-002 has no grace period.
